--TEST--
A `PropertyObserver` is not reached by an access inside the observed property's own hooks, is not reached by a `static` property at all, and is re-entered with no guard when its own body writes a property of its class — `rule:classes/property-observer`, `rule:classes/property-observer-pipeline` and `rule:classes/property-hooks`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: PropertyObserver
// `rule:classes/property-observer-pipeline` § 3's three boundaries, which all follow from the
// pipeline being written about a *receiver*: the backing slot reached from
// inside a hook, class storage that has no receiver, and an observer body that
// is itself an access. The sibling case pins what the observer is told; this
// one pins the three places it is not told anything, and the one place it is
// told about its own writing.
final class Tally {
    public static int $reads = 0;
    public static int $writes = 0;
}

// The first boundary: inside `celsius`'s own `get` and `set`, `$this->celsius`
// names the backing slot, so the store the hook commits and the load the hook
// answers with reach nothing. Without that, one write would be reported twice.
class Reading implements PropertyObserver {
    // The second boundary, on the observing class itself: a `static` property is
    // class storage with no receiving instance, so neither of its accesses below
    // reaches the pipeline at all.
    public static int $calibrations = 0;

    public int $celsius {
        get => $this->celsius;
        set(int $c) {
            $this->celsius = $c;
        }
    }

    public function constructor() {
        $this->celsius = 0;
    }

    public function onPropertyGet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
        Tally::$reads = Tally::$reads + 1;
        echo "  get ", $name, " = ", $value, "\n";
    }

    public function onPropertySet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
        Tally::$writes = Tally::$writes + 1;
        echo "  set ", $name, " = ", $value, "\n";
    }
}

// The third boundary: an observer that writes a property of its own class is
// entered again for that write, exactly as any method calling itself is. There
// is no re-entry guard, so the observer's own body is what has to stop — here
// the class storage counting the reports, which reaches nothing itself and so
// cannot be the thing it is counting.
class Escalating implements PropertyObserver {
    public static int $reports = 0;
    public int $step;

    public function constructor() {
        $this->step = 0;
    }

    public function onPropertyGet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
    }

    public function onPropertySet(string $name, mixed $value): void {
        Escalating::$reports = Escalating::$reports + 1;
        echo "  report ", Escalating::$reports, ": ", $name, " = ", $value, "\n";
        if (Escalating::$reports < 4) {
            $this->step = Escalating::$reports;
        }
    }
}

// Two accesses on the receiver — the constructor's write and this one — plus one
// read, and the hooks add four accesses of their own on top of them.
echo "a hooked write and read:\n";
Reading $r = new Reading();
$r->celsius = 21;
int $seen = $r->celsius;
echo "  caller sees ", $seen, "\n";

echo "class storage:\n";
Reading::$calibrations = 3;
echo "  caller sees ", Reading::$calibrations, "\n";

// The count is the invariant and not any one line above it: one report per
// access the receiver was named in, and not one from a hook's own slot or from
// the class storage.
echo "reads=", Tally::$reads, " writes=", Tally::$writes, "\n";

echo "an observer that writes its own class:\n";
Escalating $e = new Escalating();

// Each write commits before its report, so the deepest report is the last value
// committed and the slot keeps it after the nesting unwinds.
echo "  stored ", $e->step, "\n";
echo "  reports=", Escalating::$reports, "\n";
--EXPECT--
a hooked write and read:
  set celsius = 0
  set celsius = 21
  get celsius = 21
  caller sees 21
class storage:
  caller sees 3
reads=1 writes=2
an observer that writes its own class:
  report 1: step = 0
  report 2: step = 1
  report 3: step = 2
  report 4: step = 3
  stored 3
  reports=4
